WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.815
This explainer answers
a question architects

2
00:00:02.815 --> 00:00:05.225
hit as soon as workloads
move to the cloud.

3
00:00:05.225 --> 00:00:11.223
If we already run Infoblox Trinzic
appliances on premises, can they handle DNS,

4
00:00:11.223 --> 00:00:17.037
DHCP and IP address management for AWS,
Azure and Google Cloud by themselves?

5
00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:19.900
Or do we need to add some
form of cloud-based DDI?

6
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.466
It's built for someone still learning DDI,

7
00:00:22.466 --> 00:00:25.712
so every term is explained
the first time it appears.

8
00:00:25.712 --> 00:00:27.429
We start with the vocabulary,

9
00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:31.150
then what Trinzic does well and
where it stops at the cloud edge.

10
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.896
Then three options: Infoblox
virtual appliances in each cloud,

11
00:00:35.896 --> 00:00:40.825
Infoblox's cloud service, and native
cloud DNS alongside the Grid.

12
00:00:40.825 --> 00:00:43.564
Then the rules that keep
answers consistent,

13
00:00:43.564 --> 00:00:46.600
a decision table, and where
this fits the workbook.

14
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.400
This is research under review.

15
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.591
Every fact comes from vendor or government
documentation opened on October

16
00:00:53.591 --> 00:00:56.593
twenty-second, 2026,
and recommendations are

17
00:00:56.593 --> 00:00:59.675
labeled as design
guidance, not endorsements.

18
00:00:59.675 --> 00:01:07.498
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x, vNIOS and
Universal DDI documentation; AWS, Microsoft

19
00:01:07.498 --> 00:01:15.800
Learn and Google Cloud documentation;
FedRAMP Marketplace products.json; Book 08.

20
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.338
First, the words you need.

21
00:01:18.338 --> 00:01:23.025
DDI stands for DNS, DHCP and IPAM.

22
00:01:23.025 --> 00:01:26.112
DNS turns names into addresses.

23
00:01:26.112 --> 00:01:30.150
DHCP hands addresses to devices
when they join a network.

24
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.376
And IPAM, IP address management,

25
00:01:32.376 --> 00:01:36.350
is the record of which address
ranges exist and who owns them.

26
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.333
An authoritative server holds
the official answer for a zone,

27
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.301
a block of names such as
corp dot agency dot gov.

28
00:01:43.301 --> 00:01:47.362
A resolver is the server your
computer asks; it finds the answer.

29
00:01:47.362 --> 00:01:50.243
A Grid is two or more
Infoblox appliances that

30
00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.350
share one database and are
managed from one place.

31
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.923
That place is the Grid Master,
which holds the master copy,

32
00:01:56.923 --> 00:02:01.713
serves the console, and is the only
appliance that can sign zones with DNSSEC.

33
00:02:01.713 --> 00:02:03.812
Every other appliance is a member.

34
00:02:03.812 --> 00:02:09.362
Trinzic is Infoblox's physical
appliance line; the current one is X6.

35
00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:12.876
vNIOS is the same software
as a virtual machine,

36
00:02:12.876 --> 00:02:17.562
including in AWS, Azure and Google
Cloud, and it can join the Grid.

37
00:02:17.562 --> 00:02:22.840
NIOS-X is a newer kind of server,
managed from Infoblox's cloud service,

38
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.099
Universal DDI, whose web
console is the Infoblox Portal.

39
00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:28.325
The cloud words.

40
00:02:28.325 --> 00:02:33.793
In AWS, the resolver built into
every virtual private cloud,

41
00:02:33.793 --> 00:02:37.587
or VPC, is now called
Route 53 VPC Resolver.

42
00:02:37.587 --> 00:02:40.534
Its endpoints are
addresses that let queries

43
00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:42.938
come in from outside, or go out by rules.

44
00:02:42.938 --> 00:02:47.481
A private hosted zone in AWS, or
a private zone in Azure or Google,

45
00:02:47.481 --> 00:02:50.975
is a zone that only linked
cloud networks can see.

46
00:02:50.975 --> 00:02:54.437
VPC IPAM is AWS's address planner.

47
00:02:54.437 --> 00:02:59.663
And Azure DNS Private Resolver is
Azure's version of those endpoints.

48
00:02:59.663 --> 00:03:03.064
Last, split-brain: two systems
each believe they own a

49
00:03:03.064 --> 00:03:06.625
name and give different
answers depending on who asks.

50
00:03:06.625 --> 00:03:08.750
Avoiding it is the point of this deck.

51
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:11.295
Sources: Research glossary:

52
00:03:11.295 --> 00:03:17.712
Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x About Grids and
Grid Master Candidate; vNIOS for AWS,

53
00:03:17.712 --> 00:03:22.801
Azure and Google Cloud; Universal
DDI What's New (renames);

54
00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:29.107
AWS What is Route 53 VPC Resolver,
private hosted zones, What is IPAM;

55
00:03:29.107 --> 00:03:36.550
Microsoft Learn Azure DNS Private
Resolver; Google Cloud DNS best practices.

56
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:42.146
On premises, a Trinzic Grid is
the agency's DNS and DHCP servers

57
00:03:42.146 --> 00:03:46.612
plus its address record, all
managed from the Grid Master.

58
00:03:46.612 --> 00:03:50.826
Changes made there replicate to
every member over encrypted tunnels.

59
00:03:50.826 --> 00:03:52.337
It does several things well.

60
00:03:52.337 --> 00:03:55.762
DNS views let one Grid
give internal clients one

61
00:03:55.762 --> 00:03:59.101
answer and outside clients
another, on purpose.

62
00:03:59.101 --> 00:04:03.429
Response policy zones, which
Infoblox calls DNS Firewall,

63
00:04:03.429 --> 00:04:09.200
block or redirect bad names, up to thirty-two
of them, with hits logged to syslog.

64
00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.371
DNSSEC signing is centralized:

65
00:04:11.371 --> 00:04:14.960
only the Grid Master can be
primary for a signed zone,

66
00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.136
and keys live on the Grid Master or on
an external hardware security module,

67
00:04:20.136 --> 00:04:23.225
a Thales Luna or Entrust
nShield, but not both.

68
00:04:23.225 --> 00:04:27.084
One caution: the default
algorithm is RSA with SHA-1,

69
00:04:27.084 --> 00:04:30.300
which a federal deployment should change.

70
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.188
DHCP runs as failover pairs.

71
00:04:33.188 --> 00:04:39.088
And the Grid can manage existing Windows
DNS and DHCP servers without agents.

72
00:04:39.088 --> 00:04:40.275
Why does that matter?

73
00:04:40.275 --> 00:04:44.383
Book 08 of the workbook argues
for one DDI policy plane:

74
00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:49.405
a single system of record for zones,
forwarding rules, address data

75
00:04:49.405 --> 00:04:54.700
and security policy, with cloud DNS
managed as an overlay, not replaced.

76
00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.262
On premises, the Grid fits that role.

77
00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:59.313
The question is the cloud edge.

78
00:04:59.313 --> 00:05:03.512
And note that on-premises appliances
aren't a FedRAMP cloud service;

79
00:05:03.512 --> 00:05:06.062
they sit inside the agency's own boundary.

80
00:05:06.062 --> 00:05:12.228
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x:
About Grids, Configuring DNS Views,

81
00:05:12.228 --> 00:05:18.031
About Infoblox DNS Firewall,
Configuring DNSSEC on a Grid,

82
00:05:18.031 --> 00:05:26.614
Setting DNSSEC Parameters, DHCP Failover,
About Managing Microsoft Windows Servers;

83
00:05:26.614 --> 00:05:31.692
Book 08, Chapter 08, What a
DDI policy plane is made of;

84
00:05:31.692 --> 00:05:37.740
FedRAMP Marketplace products.json
(FR2017257053 scope).

85
00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.365
It can see.

86
00:05:39.365 --> 00:05:43.644
vDiscovery logs in to AWS,
Azure, Google Cloud or VMware

87
00:05:43.644 --> 00:05:48.303
on a schedule and pulls back
networks and virtual machines.

88
00:05:48.303 --> 00:05:53.115
AWS jobs have a GovCloud option, and
Azure jobs cover Azure Government.

89
00:05:53.115 --> 00:05:55.796
Discovered subnets
become managed networks,

90
00:05:55.796 --> 00:06:00.453
and the Grid can create records for
discovered machines in zones it already has.

91
00:06:00.453 --> 00:06:03.064
It's a scheduled pull, not a live feed.

92
00:06:03.064 --> 00:06:05.065
It can also import cloud zones.

93
00:06:05.065 --> 00:06:11.320
With a Cloud Network Automation license,
NIOS copies Route 53, Azure DNS and Google

94
00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:17.115
Cloud DNS zones into the Grid, so
on-premises clients can resolve cloud names.

95
00:06:17.115 --> 00:06:18.528
The import is one way.

96
00:06:18.528 --> 00:06:22.500
For Route 53, Infoblox says
the sync runs into NIOS,

97
00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.539
not vice versa, and NIOS edits to
those records are overwritten.

98
00:06:27.539 --> 00:06:28.990
Now what it can't control.

99
00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.940
It can't create or own
a cloud private zone.

100
00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:36.584
No NIOS document describes
managing Route 53 VPC Resolver

101
00:06:36.584 --> 00:06:40.565
endpoints or rules, or
Azure's forwarding rulesets.

102
00:06:40.565 --> 00:06:47.140
It can't be the outside authority for AWS
VPC IPAM; that's a Universal DDI feature.

103
00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.240
And it can't run DHCP for cloud subnets.

104
00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.378
AWS hands out addresses
from its own DHCP servers.

105
00:06:54.378 --> 00:06:58.011
Google limits vNIOS DHCP
to on-premises networks,

106
00:06:58.011 --> 00:07:03.328
and Azure doesn't support running DHCP
in Azure for on-premises networks.

107
00:07:03.328 --> 00:07:08.046
So, as an inference: in the cloud,
Infoblox's job is DNS and IPAM,

108
00:07:08.046 --> 00:07:12.578
and the cloud's resolver still
answers cloud private zones.

109
00:07:12.578 --> 00:07:18.742
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x Selecting
the Endpoint Server, vDiscovery AWS,

110
00:07:18.742 --> 00:07:24.905
Guidelines for vDiscovery Jobs, Creating
DNS Records for Discovered VMs,

111
00:07:24.905 --> 00:07:30.362
Cloud Network Automation; Amazon
Route 53 Integration Overview;

112
00:07:30.362 --> 00:07:37.233
Integrating Azure DNS and Google Cloud
DNS with NIOS and their limitations pages;

113
00:07:37.233 --> 00:07:44.360
AWS DHCP option sets; Limitations of vNIOS
for Google Cloud; About vNIOS for Azure.

114
00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:48.473
Option A puts vNIOS
members inside each cloud,

115
00:07:48.473 --> 00:07:51.748
as Grid members and
high-availability pairs.

116
00:07:51.748 --> 00:07:56.334
Infoblox's AWS reference design
puts the pair in a shared-services

117
00:07:56.334 --> 00:07:59.836
hub VPC that syncs with the
on-premises Grid Master.

118
00:07:59.836 --> 00:08:03.009
Signed zones stay signed:
the members serve them,

119
00:08:03.009 --> 00:08:05.310
but the keys stay on the Grid Master.

120
00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.948
How do cloud workloads reach vNIOS?

121
00:08:07.948 --> 00:08:09.672
Two documented patterns.

122
00:08:09.672 --> 00:08:13.936
Pattern one: point the
network's DNS at vNIOS.

123
00:08:13.936 --> 00:08:21.110
In AWS that's a DHCP option set; in Azure,
the virtual network's custom DNS setting.

124
00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:22.773
Simple, with a catch.

125
00:08:22.773 --> 00:08:25.704
In Azure, queries then
go straight to vNIOS,

126
00:08:25.704 --> 00:08:28.972
and Azure private zones
aren't consulted first.

127
00:08:28.972 --> 00:08:36.507
So vNIOS has to forward Azure-private
names back to Azure DNS at 168.63.129.16,

128
00:08:36.507 --> 00:08:40.760
or to a Private Resolver inbound endpoint.

129
00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:46.322
Pattern two: keep the cloud's own resolver
and forward chosen domains to vNIOS.

130
00:08:46.322 --> 00:08:50.930
In AWS, that's an outbound endpoint
and one forwarding rule per domain,

131
00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.598
and rules can be shared across accounts.

132
00:08:53.598 --> 00:08:57.272
In Azure, it's an outbound
endpoint and a forwarding ruleset,

133
00:08:57.272 --> 00:08:58.997
and clients change nothing.

134
00:08:58.997 --> 00:09:02.585
In Google, it's a forwarding
zone, which Google prefers.

135
00:09:02.585 --> 00:09:04.510
One AWS caution.

136
00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:09.252
If vNIOS has to resolve Route 53
private zones for other networks,

137
00:09:09.252 --> 00:09:11.535
forward to an inbound endpoint.

138
00:09:11.535 --> 00:09:14.984
AWS says forwarding to a
VPC's built-in plus-two

139
00:09:14.984 --> 00:09:19.160
resolver address from outside
that VPC isn't supported.

140
00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:24.433
And every vNIOS member is agency-operated
and inside the agency's boundary;

141
00:09:24.433 --> 00:09:26.985
no FedRAMP authorization covers it.

142
00:09:26.985 --> 00:09:33.026
Sources: Infoblox About vNIOS for
AWS, Shared-Services VPC Deployments,

143
00:09:33.026 --> 00:09:38.862
About vNIOS for Azure, Configure
vNIOS as Primary DNS for Azure VNets,

144
00:09:38.862 --> 00:09:45.005
vNIOS for Google Cloud; AWS DHCP option
sets, What is Route 53 VPC Resolver,

145
00:09:45.005 --> 00:09:51.763
Forwarding outbound queries, Resolving
queries between VPCs and your network;

146
00:09:51.763 --> 00:09:57.189
Microsoft Learn DNS Private
Resolver and VNet name resolution;

147
00:09:57.189 --> 00:10:03.290
Google Cloud DNS best practices;
research_platform (DNSSEC on a Grid).

148
00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:07.019
Option B joins the Grid to Universal DDI,

149
00:10:07.019 --> 00:10:10.540
Infoblox's software-as-a-service
control plane.

150
00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:13.898
You add a join token from the
Infoblox Portal to the Grid,

151
00:10:13.898 --> 00:10:17.402
then turn on IPAM Federation
and Cloud Grid Management.

152
00:10:17.402 --> 00:10:24.752
Federation, which needs NIOS 9.0.4 or later,
keeps address space unique everywhere.

153
00:10:24.752 --> 00:10:28.290
The Grid stays primary for its DNS data.

154
00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:29.302
What does it add?

155
00:10:29.302 --> 00:10:34.740
It's the only documented Infoblox
mechanism that writes back to cloud DNS.

156
00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:39.810
It syncs Route 53, Azure DNS
and Google Cloud DNS read-write,

157
00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:45.302
and it discovers resolver endpoints
and rules in AWS and Azure.

158
00:10:45.302 --> 00:10:48.513
Cloud forwarders take
queries in from a cloud,

159
00:10:48.513 --> 00:10:51.640
and cloud to cloud only
between AWS and Azure.

160
00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:55.277
And on commercial AWS,
Infoblox can be the outside

161
00:10:55.277 --> 00:10:59.802
authority for a VPC IPAM private
scope, on the Advanced Tier.

162
00:10:59.802 --> 00:11:01.439
The limits are documented.

163
00:11:01.439 --> 00:11:05.640
Route 53 sync doesn't support
non-simple routing policies.

164
00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:09.215
Azure and Google private zones
can't be created from the portal.

165
00:11:09.215 --> 00:11:11.040
DNSSEC records aren't synced.

166
00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.687
And the portal can't promote a Grid
Master Candidate or manage DHCP failover,

167
00:11:15.687 --> 00:11:18.490
so the Grid Master stays
the recovery console.

168
00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:19.640
Two more notes.

169
00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.576
The cloud forwarding and private-zone
features run on NIOS-X servers,

170
00:11:24.576 --> 00:11:28.040
not classic Trinzic, so
you'd need NIOS-X capacity.

171
00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:31.719
And the government edition:
Infoblox Government Cloud

172
00:11:31.719 --> 00:11:35.478
is FedRAMP Certified at
Moderate, hosted in AWS GovCloud.

173
00:11:35.478 --> 00:11:41.115
Its cloud discovery is limited: single
AWS accounts and no Azure tenants.

174
00:11:41.115 --> 00:11:44.522
The VPC IPAM handoff isn't
in its release notes,

175
00:11:44.522 --> 00:11:48.015
and AWS excludes GovCloud
from that integration.

176
00:11:48.015 --> 00:11:50.065
Confirm the rest with Infoblox.

177
00:11:50.065 --> 00:11:55.250
Sources: Infoblox Onboarding NIOS
Services, NIOS Grid Connector,

178
