185
00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:43.906
Option C keeps the Grid
authoritative for agency zones

186
00:12:43.906 --> 00:12:47.918
and lets each cloud's native
DNS own its own private zones.

187
00:12:47.918 --> 00:12:51.954
The two connect by conditional
forwarding: a rule that says,

188
00:12:51.954 --> 00:12:55.167
for names ending in this
domain, ask that server.

189
00:12:55.167 --> 00:13:00.866
Inward: the Grid forwards cloud domains
to an inbound endpoint in AWS or Azure,

190
00:13:00.866 --> 00:13:04.428
or to an inbound server
policy address in Google,

191
00:13:04.428 --> 00:13:07.455
over Direct Connect, ExpressRoute or VPN.

192
00:13:07.455 --> 00:13:12.718
Outward: each cloud forwards agency
domains to the Grid, with an outbound

193
00:13:12.718 --> 00:13:18.068
endpoint and rules in AWS, a ruleset in
Azure, or a forwarding zone in Google.

194
00:13:18.068 --> 00:13:19.031
Facts to know.

195
00:13:19.031 --> 00:13:22.776
A Route 53 private hosted
zone answers only inside

196
00:13:22.776 --> 00:13:26.255
its linked VPCs or through
an inbound endpoint.

197
00:13:26.255 --> 00:13:30.693
Asked from anywhere else, the name
resolves on the public internet instead.

198
00:13:30.693 --> 00:13:36.266
In Azure, a network using Azure-provided
DNS checks linked private zones first,

199
00:13:36.266 --> 00:13:39.181
then forwarding rulesets, then Azure DNS.

200
00:13:39.181 --> 00:13:43.982
Google recommends exactly this
design: two authoritative systems,

201
00:13:43.982 --> 00:13:47.068
conditional forwarding, no zone transfers.

202
00:13:47.068 --> 00:13:50.582
In GovCloud, Route 53 public
and private DNS work,

203
00:13:50.582 --> 00:13:56.293
and Resolver endpoints and rules are
available, but Resolver delegation isn't.

204
00:13:56.293 --> 00:13:58.217
Addresses follow the same idea.

205
00:13:58.217 --> 00:14:02.667
The Grid owns the agency's address
blocks and hands each cloud a block.

206
00:14:02.667 --> 00:14:07.855
On commercial AWS, that handoff can
be automated through Universal DDI.

207
00:14:07.855 --> 00:14:12.389
In GovCloud, Azure and Google, it's
manual: reserve the block in the Grid,

208
00:14:12.389 --> 00:14:17.242
create the cloud pool with that range,
and let vDiscovery check the result.

209
00:14:17.242 --> 00:14:23.729
Sources: AWS Working with private hosted
zones, What is Route 53 VPC Resolver,

210
00:14:23.729 --> 00:14:27.333
Forwarding inbound and outbound queries,

211
00:14:27.333 --> 00:14:34.026
Resolving queries between VPCs and your
network, GovCloud Route 53 user guide,

212
00:14:34.026 --> 00:14:40.513
Route 53 endpoints; Microsoft Learn DNS
Private Resolver, Azure Private DNS,

213
00:14:40.513 --> 00:14:46.073
AVNM IPAM; Google Cloud DNS best
practices and server policies;

214
00:14:46.073 --> 00:14:50.912
AWS Integrate VPC IPAM with
Infoblox and What's New Nov.

215
00:14:50.912 --> 00:14:51.890
17, 2025.

216
00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:56.065
Whatever option you pick, four
rules prevent split-brain.

217
00:14:56.065 --> 00:14:59.503
One: every zone has
exactly one writable owner.

218
00:14:59.503 --> 00:15:05.727
That's the Grid, Route 53, Azure
Private DNS or Cloud DNS, never two.

219
00:15:05.727 --> 00:15:08.702
Everything else reaches
the owner by forwarding,

220
00:15:08.702 --> 00:15:11.315
or holds a copy that is
explicitly read-only.

221
00:15:11.315 --> 00:15:14.347
NIOS overwrites edits to
imported cloud zones,

222
00:15:14.347 --> 00:15:18.390
and portal-owned resolver rules
overwrite cloud-side changes.

223
00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.977
So decide ownership
before you turn sync on.

224
00:15:20.977 --> 00:15:23.702
Two: divide the namespace by owner.

225
00:15:23.702 --> 00:15:27.664
For example, corp dot agency
dot gov lives on the Grid,

226
00:15:27.664 --> 00:15:33.191
and aws dot corp dot agency dot gov
lives in a Route 53 private hosted zone.

227
00:15:33.191 --> 00:15:36.843
On-premises resolvers
forward the child to AWS,

228
00:15:36.843 --> 00:15:39.840
and AWS forwards the parent to the Grid.

229
00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.511
AWS also allows inbound delegation,

230
00:15:42.511 --> 00:15:46.428
but Resolver delegation
isn't available in GovCloud.

231
00:15:46.428 --> 00:15:49.643
And don't create a cloud
private zone with the same name

232
00:15:49.643 --> 00:15:53.002
as a Grid zone unless it's a
deliberate split-horizon view.

233
00:15:53.002 --> 00:15:58.165
Three: DNSSEC keys stay on the Grid
Master or its hardware security module.

234
00:15:58.165 --> 00:16:03.505
Route 53 can't sign private hosted zones,
and Universal DDI doesn't sync DNSSEC

235
00:16:03.505 --> 00:16:08.427
records, so don't sign in one system
and manage through sync from another.

236
00:16:08.427 --> 00:16:11.149
Validation belongs on agency resolvers;

237
00:16:11.149 --> 00:16:14.191
the Azure-provided
resolver doesn't validate.

238
00:16:14.191 --> 00:16:17.015
Four: IPAM authority stays on the Grid.

239
00:16:17.015 --> 00:16:18.937
Clouds get delegated blocks,

240
00:16:18.937 --> 00:16:23.502
and one network view for routable
space lets the Grid catch overlaps.

241
00:16:23.502 --> 00:16:30.775
Sources: Research synthesis, Avoiding
split-brain, DNSSEC keys and IPAM authority:

242
00:16:30.775 --> 00:16:34.724
Infoblox Route 53, Azure
DNS and GCP sync pages;

243
00:16:34.724 --> 00:16:41.270
Discovering Cloud Forwarder Endpoints;
AWS private hosted zones, Resolver,

244
00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:44.180
GovCloud Route 53, DNSSEC signing;

245
00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:50.830
Microsoft Learn DNS Private Resolver
and Azure DNSSEC; Google Cloud DNS best

246
00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:57.430
practices; Infoblox DNSSEC on a Grid
and Network Views; Book 08, Chapter 02.

247
00:16:58.180 --> 00:16:59.517
The decision table.

248
00:16:59.517 --> 00:17:02.576
Every row is design
guidance, not an endorsement,

249
00:17:02.576 --> 00:17:04.717
resting on the facts we've covered.

250
00:17:04.717 --> 00:17:09.180
Mostly on premises, with a small
cloud footprint: Trinzic with native

251
00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:13.956
cloud resolvers and conditional
forwarding, the minimal form of Option C.

252
00:17:13.956 --> 00:17:17.585
It's the lowest cost, needs
no new SaaS authorization,

253
00:17:17.585 --> 00:17:20.117
and NIOS can't own cloud zones anyway.

254
00:17:20.117 --> 00:17:24.087
Hybrid with one cloud,
commercial or government:

255
00:17:24.087 --> 00:17:28.155
add an HA vNIOS pair in a
hub network, Option A plus C.

256
00:17:28.155 --> 00:17:32.836
It keeps resolution local if the WAN
fails and extends response policy,

257
00:17:32.836 --> 00:17:35.692
signed zones and Grid IPAM into the cloud.

258
00:17:35.692 --> 00:17:39.867
vNIOS is documented for both
GovCloud and Azure Government.

259
00:17:39.867 --> 00:17:42.627
Multi-cloud at FedRAMP Moderate and below:

260
00:17:42.627 --> 00:17:47.042
add Universal DDI on top, through
Government Cloud for federal work.

261
00:17:47.042 --> 00:17:51.026
It's the only Infoblox mechanism
that writes to cloud DNS,

262
00:17:51.026 --> 00:17:53.568
within the Government Cloud limits.

263
00:17:53.568 --> 00:17:56.902
GovCloud, Azure Government, FedRAMP High,

264
00:17:56.902 --> 00:18:01.806
or Defense Impact Levels 4 and 5:
stay with options A and C only.

265
00:18:01.806 --> 00:18:07.372
Infoblox's SaaS is Moderate-only, the
VPC IPAM integration excludes GovCloud,

266
00:18:07.372 --> 00:18:11.168
and native services carry
the higher authorizations.

267
00:18:11.168 --> 00:18:17.392
Google lists Cloud DNS for High,
IL4 and IL5 under Assured Workloads.

268
00:18:17.392 --> 00:18:22.705
For Azure Government, confirm DNS
Private Resolver is available first.

269
00:18:22.705 --> 00:18:27.964
In short, the design guidance is: Trinzic
plus native cloud resolvers as the

270
00:18:27.964 --> 00:18:33.393
baseline, with vNIOS and Universal DDI
added only where they earn their place.

271
00:18:33.393 --> 00:18:38.642
